usage.examples:
A standalone primary action:

```html
<ki-button variant="primary">Save</ki-button>
```

In momentary mode `type` defaults to `submit`. Use `type="button"` for an independent action inside a form.

```html
<form>
  <ki-button type="button">Cancel</ki-button>
  <ki-button variant="primary">Save changes</ki-button>
</form>
```

Use `toggle` for a persistent action. `selected` sets the starting state and each activation changes `aria-pressed` while keeping the label. Toggle mode acts independently of form submission and reset, regardless of `type`.

```html
<ki-button id="pin" variant="outline" toggle selected>Pin</ki-button>
<script type="module">
  const button = document.getElementById('pin');
  button.addEventListener('ki-change', ({ detail: { selected } }) => {
    console.log('Pinned', selected);
  });
</script>
```

Programmatic writes to `selected` are silent. A parent coordinating several toggles can listen for their bubbling `ki-change` events and update their `selected` values together.

Open a dialog with `command="show-modal"` and its ID in `commandfor`. The
dialog and invoker share a document or shadow root. The command takes
precedence over form submission or reset.

```html
<ki-button commandfor="draft-preview" command="show-modal">Preview draft</ki-button>
<ki-dialog id="draft-preview" heading="Draft preview">
  This draft is visible only to you.
  <ki-button slot="footer" commandfor="draft-preview" command="close">Done</ki-button>
</ki-dialog>
```

Programmatic creation after registration; string attributes and properties are interchangeable:

```ts
import { defineCustomElement } from '@kimen/elements/ki-button';

defineCustomElement();

const saveButton = document.createElement('ki-button');
saveButton.setAttribute('type', 'button');
saveButton.textContent = 'Save';
document.body.append(saveButton);
```

While the action a button started is in progress, set `loading` on it instead of `disabled`: it keeps its width and focus, ignores further activation (it neither submits nor resets its form), draws a spinner in place of its label and announces `loading-label` (default "Loading") as a polite status message. Clear it when the action ends:

```html
<form id="profile">
  <ki-button variant="primary" loading-label="Saving">Save changes</ki-button>
</form>
<script type="module">
  const form = document.getElementById('profile');
  const save = form.querySelector('ki-button');
  form.addEventListener('submit', async (event) => {
    event.preventDefault();
    save.loading = true;
    try {
      await fetch('/profile', { method: 'POST', body: new FormData(form) });
    } finally {
      save.loading = false;
    }
  });
</script>
```

# Confirm an assistant action

```html
<ki-confirmation
  id="send-email-prompt"
  heading="Send this email?"
  action="send_email"
  approve-label="Send email"
  deny-label="Keep draft"
  consequence="Recipients will get the message immediately."
  remember
  remember-label="Allow sending email for the rest of this conversation"
>
  <dl>
    <dt>To</dt><dd>Ana Rivera</dd>
    <dt>Subject</dt><dd>Weekly report</dd>
  </dl>
</ki-confirmation>
```

Map the two decisions in trusted host code. This MCP Apps example uses a
ready `SurfaceBridge` whose rendered spec declares `approve-send-email` and
`deny-send-email` in its top-level `actions`. Keep a guarded node's scalar
`action` binding unset because the component declares two events. Its
`props.action` carries the visible tool identifier. A trusted host that
applies an origin allowlist can supply that identifier after rendering, since
guarded spec data under that prop name passes through the URL policy.

```js
const prompt = document.querySelector('#send-email-prompt');

prompt.addEventListener('ki-approve', (event) => {
  if (event.composedPath()[0] !== prompt) return;
  bridge.dispatch('approve-send-email', {
    tool: 'send_email',
    remember: event.detail?.remember === true,
  });
});

prompt.addEventListener('ki-deny', (event) => {
  if (event.composedPath()[0] !== prompt) return;
  bridge.dispatch('deny-send-email', { tool: 'send_email' });
});
```

The host owns execution, deadlines and any conversation permission. Supply
outcome metadata and set the resolved state after recording the decision.

```js
prompt.resolvedBy = 'You';
prompt.resolvedAt = '10:32';
prompt.state = 'approved';
```

Call `await prompt.focusPrompt()` when the person is waiting for the request.
Keep the proposed inputs stable while the person is deciding. Mount a new
element if another request for the same tool replaces a pending one.

# ki-conversation examples

## Bounded transcript

Set a block size to make the transcript scroll. Enable `stick-to-bottom` to
follow new messages and keep incoming replies in view.

```html
<ki-conversation id="help-chat" label="Component help" stick-to-bottom style="block-size: 20rem">
  <ki-message message-role="user">Which inputs handle form errors?</ki-message>
  <ki-message message-role="event" icon-name="search">Searched the component docs</ki-message>
  <ki-message message-role="assistant">Input, textarea and select show field errors.</ki-message>
</ki-conversation>
<label for="prompt">Message</label>
<textarea id="prompt"></textarea>
```

## Pass application chunks to a message

Call this function with the async iterable supplied by your application.
Keep the composer and any waiting indicator outside the live log.

```js
async function appendReply(conversation, chunks) {
  const reply = document.createElement('ki-message');
  reply.setAttribute('streaming', '');
  conversation.append(reply);
  try {
    for await (const chunk of chunks) {
      reply.append(chunk);
    }
  } finally {
    reply.removeAttribute('streaming');
  }
}
```

## Custom-elements entry points

```ts
import { defineCustomElement as defineConversation } from '@kimen/elements/ki-conversation';
import { defineCustomElement as defineMessage } from '@kimen/elements/ki-message';

defineConversation();
defineMessage();
```

# Using ki-feature-icon

Load the token stylesheet, register each custom element, and import the
Lucide glyph you use. Place one glyph with its default inherited size
in the slot, next to the heading that names the section.

```js
import '@kimen/tokens/css';
import '@kimen/elements/icons/shield-check';
import { defineCustomElement as defineFeatureIcon } from '@kimen/elements/ki-feature-icon';
import { defineCustomElement as defineIcon } from '@kimen/elements/ki-icon';

defineFeatureIcon();
defineIcon();
```

```html
<div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:var(--ki-space-md)">
  <ki-feature-icon tone="primary-light" size="md">
    <ki-icon name="shield-check"></ki-icon>
  </ki-feature-icon>
  <h2>Security settings</h2>
</div>
```

Choose a circle with `shape="circle"`. For an inline SVG, use `currentColor`
for the glyph's stroke or fill. Keep controls and meaningful labels outside
the tile so they can receive focus and be announced.

A guarded catalog surface supplies the glyph as a `ki-icon` node in the
default slot. The host imports the glyph before rendering the surface.

```json
{
  "version": 1,
  "root": {
    "component": "div",
    "props": { "direction": "row", "align": "center", "gap": "md" },
    "slots": {
      "": [
        {
          "component": "ki-feature-icon",
          "props": { "tone": "primary-light" },
          "slots": {
            "": [{ "component": "ki-icon", "props": { "name": "shield-check" } }]
          }
        },
        { "component": "h2", "slots": { "": ["Security settings"] } }
      ]
    }
  }
}
```

props.docs:
Completed amount. Presentation and ARIA clamp this value to `0..max`;
malformed values fall back to `0`. Ignored while `indeterminate` is set.
When to use: set with `max` for determinate task advancement.
When NOT to use: do not set a fabricated value for unknown-duration work;
set `indeterminate` instead.

